<Guide slug="india-monsoon" photo="ladakh">
  <p>
    India’s southwest monsoon brings around three-quarters of the country’s annual rain in four months. It usually reaches the
    Kerala coast around <strong>1 June</strong>, Mumbai around mid-June and Delhi in late June, covering the whole country by early
    July. It begins to withdraw from Rajasthan in September and has left most of India by mid-October. Tamil Nadu and the far
    southeast get their main rains later, from the northeast monsoon in October–December.
  </p>
  <p>
    Monsoon travel has its charms — dramatic skies, green landscapes, low prices — but heavy rain, flooding and landslides can
    disrupt plans, especially in the mountains. Here’s how our Indian destinations rate, and where to go instead.
  </p>

  <h2>India during the monsoon</h2>
